WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.205
Signed integer overflow in ordinary C
arithmetic is undefined behavior.

2
00:00:04.285 --> 00:00:07.631
That means a program should not depend on
a signed result wrapping,

3
00:00:07.711 --> 00:00:12.133
saturating, trapping, or producing any
particular value when the mathematical result

4
00:00:12.213 --> 00:00:14.005
is outside the signed type's range.

5
00:00:14.085 --> 00:00:18.029
Defensive code checks before the operation
when the result must remain valid.

6
00:00:18.109 --> 00:00:19.902
Unsigned arithmetic is different.

7
00:00:19.982 --> 00:00:23.866
It is defined modulo the number of values
representable by the unsigned type.

8
00:00:23.946 --> 00:00:27.411
That definition can be useful for
deliberate low-level arithmetic,

9
00:00:27.491 --> 00:00:31.196
but defined wraparound can still be a bug
when the program expects a count,

10
00:00:31.276 --> 00:00:35.280
length, offset, limit, or allocation size
to keep increasing in normal numeric

11
00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:35.718
order.

12
00:00:35.798 --> 00:00:40.160
Integer promotions can change smaller
integer types before arithmetic is performed.

13
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.587
A calculation involving char, signed char,
unsigned char, short,

14
00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:46.654
or unsigned short may be promoted before
the operator runs.

15
00:00:46.734 --> 00:00:50.917
Reviewers should not assume arithmetic
happens in the apparent storage type of the

16
00:00:50.997 --> 00:00:53.148
variable on the left side of an
assignment.

17
00:00:53.228 --> 00:00:56.873
The usual arithmetic conversions can also
change the type used for mixed

18
00:00:56.953 --> 00:00:57.670
expressions.

19
00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:00.260
When signed and unsigned values appear
together,

20
00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:04.284
a negative signed value may be converted
to an unsigned value for comparison or

21
00:01:04.364 --> 00:01:05.021
arithmetic.

22
00:01:05.101 --> 00:01:09.164
That can make a guard condition look
correct at a glance while testing a different

23
00:01:09.244 --> 00:01:11.455
relationship than the developer intended.

24
00:01:11.535 --> 00:01:15.897
Compiler optimization can rely on the
assumption that undefined signed overflow does

25
00:01:15.977 --> 00:01:16.515
not occur.

26
00:01:16.595 --> 00:01:20.479
That matters because code that tries to
check for signed overflow after it has

27
00:01:20.559 --> 00:01:22.471
already happened may not be reliable.

28
00:01:22.551 --> 00:01:26.614
The safe habit is to check preconditions
before signed arithmetic when overflow

29
00:01:26.694 --> 00:01:27.411
would matter.

30
00:01:27.491 --> 00:01:29.822
Defensive C code makes range checks
explicit.

31
00:01:29.902 --> 00:01:32.172
If addition, subtraction, multiplication,

32
00:01:32.252 --> 00:01:36.555
or conversion controls allocation,
indexing, access, quota enforcement, parsing,

33
00:01:36.635 --> 00:01:38.846
or resource limits, verify operands first.

34
00:01:38.926 --> 00:01:43.049
The result should be trusted only after
the operation is known to be representable

35
00:01:43.129 --> 00:01:45.220
and meaningful for its business purpose.
